What your client leaves on each photo: stars, and a note where they'd like a change.
How it works
Put the shoot in a collection. Select its photos in Images and add them to a collection of their own, named after the client or the session.
Share it as a gallery. Switch on rating, choose how long the link works (1, 7 or 30 days) and whether it carries small copies or the originals, then send it by email or message.
Your client picks. They open the link on any phone or computer, tap the stars on the photos they like and write a note under any of them: “a bit brighter”, “remove the sign on the left”. No account, no app.
You see their picks. Off The Cloud lets you know when ratings come in. Open the collection, show 4 stars or more, sort by rating and read each note with its photo.
What you and your client get
A link, nothing to install. The gallery opens in the browser on any phone, tablet or computer, and your client can rate straight away, without an account or an app.
Stars and a note on each photo. One to five stars for the ones they like, and a note wherever they'd like a change: brighter, a closer crop, something taken out.
An alert as ratings arrive. Off The Cloud lets you know when your client rates a photo or leaves a note through the link.
Their picks on top. Show 5 stars, 4 stars or more, the rated or unrated photos, or only those with a note, and sort by rating to see the highest rated first.
Small copies or originals. Send small copies for proofing, while the originals stay in your library, or the originals for your client to download.
A link that ends by itself. It works for 1, 7 or 30 days, and you can delete it sooner from Settings, under Shared Links.
@img.src=/img/hero-900.jpg
@img.alt=An Off The Cloud device on a desk, next to a camera and a computer showing your photo library
Your shoots stay on your own device
Off The Cloud runs on a small device you own, on your desk or in your studio: a Raspberry Pi 5 with two USB drives, each keeping a full copy of your library. Your client's gallery is served straight from it, through your own address at off-the.cloud, so the shoot is never uploaded to someone else's cloud.
Your photos are encrypted at rest on the device, and so are the notes your client leaves. The gallery itself is a copy encrypted with a key that only its link carries.
